# Foundational HTML Elements

There are _many_ different types of HTML elements---far too many to fully cover in this (or any other) course. If you want to get a sense of how many  there are, [this page contains a list of all the different types of HTML elements](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements). We will cover the most essential ones here.

## Document Structure

Pretty much every HTML document will have the same top-level structure. This is the basic structure of an HTML document:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head></head>
  <body></body>
</html>
```

- `<!DOCTYPE html>`: This tells the browser which version of HTML to use (the latest version, HTML 5).
- `<html>`: This is the "root" element of every document, meaning all other elements are contained within this element.
- `<head>`: This element contains metadata about the document, such as the title (using the `<title>` element). It is not displayed in the browser.
- `<body>`: This element contains the content of the document, such as text, images, and links. It is displayed in the browser.

In the small code examples here, we will often omit the `<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html>`, `<head>`, and `<body>` elements for brevity. However, in a real HTML document, these elements should be included.

## Content Elements

These elements are used to display text in different ways. They can be used to create headings, paragraphs, lists, and more.

- `<h1>` to `<h6>`: These are heading elements, with `<h1>` being "level 1" (the largest) and `<h6>` being "level 6" (the smallest). They are used to create headings and subheadings.
- `<p>`: This is a paragraph element. It is used to create paragraphs of text.
- `<strong>`: This element is used to create strong emphasis on text. It is typically displayed in **bold**.
- `<em>`: This element is used to create emphasis on text. It is typically displayed in _italics_.

For example:

```{html autorun}
<h1>My First Web Page</h1>
<p>This is <em>one</em> <strong>paragraph.</strong></p>
```

### Lists

- `<ul>`: This is an unordered list element. It is used to create a list of items that do not have a specific order. Each item in the list is contained within a `<li>` (list item) element.
- `<ol>`: This is an ordered list element. It is used to create a list of items that have a specific order. Each item in the list is contained within a `<li>` (list item) element.
- `<li>`: This is a list item element. It is used to create items in a list, either ordered (`ol`) or unordered (`ul`).

For example:

```{html autorun}
<p>Unordered list:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Apples</li>
    <li>Bananas</li>
    <li>Cherries</li>
</ul>
<p>Ordered list:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Preheat the oven</li>
    <li>Mix the ingredients</li>
    <li>Bake for 30 minutes</li>
</ol>
```

## Interactive Elements

These elements are used to create interactive elements, such as links and buttons.

- `<a>`: This is an anchor element. It is used to create links to other pages or sections of the same page.
  - Note that links also need to have an `href` attribute to specify the URL to link to. We will cover attributes in more detail in the next section.
- `<button>`: This element is used to create buttons that can be clicked to perform an action. It can contain text or other elements, such as images or icons.
- `<input>`: This element is used to create input fields, such as text boxes, checkboxes, and radio buttons. It can be used to collect user input.

```{html autorun}
<a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>
<button>Click me</button>
<input />
```

## Generic Containers

These elements have no _semantic_ meaning (i.e., they don't convey any specific meaning about the content they contain). They are useful for grouping elements together, applying styles, or manipulating them with JavaScript.

- `<div>`: A generic block-level container (think of "block-level" as meaning that it starts on a new line and takes up the full width). It has no semantic meaning, but is useful for grouping elements together.
- `<span>`: A generic _inline_ container (think of "inline" as meaning that it only takes up as much width as its content needs). Like a `div`, it has no semantic meaning, but is useful for grouping elements together.

```{html autorun}
The content <div>here</div> is inside a div element. Note that the div element is a block-level element, so it takes up the full width of the page.

<hr />

The content <span>here</span> is inside a span element. Note that the span element is an inline element, so it only takes up as much width as its content needs. You probably wouldn't know it was a span element if I didn't tell you, since it doesn't have any special styling by default.
```

## Exercises

1. Create a paragraph element (`p`) with the text "Apple".

```{html}

```

```+solution
<p>Apple</p>
```

```+test
   assert('p').should('exist').run('Could not find a p element', 'Created a p element')
   assert('p').should('have.text', 'Apple').run('The p element does not have the text "Apple"', 'Created a p element with the text "Apple"')
```

2. Create a button element (`button`) with the text "Submit".

```{html}

```

```+solution
<button>Submit</button>
```

```+test
   assert('button').should('exist').run('Could not find a button element', 'Created a button element')
   assert('button').should('have.text', 'Submit').run('The button element does not have the text "Submit"', 'Created a button element with the text "Submit"')
```

3. Create an emphasis element (`em`) with the text "Note".

```{html}

```

```+solution
<em>Note</em>
```

```+test
   assert('em').should('exist').run('Could not find an em element', 'Created an em element')
   assert('em').should('have.text', 'Note').run('The em element does not have the text "Note"', 'Created an em element with the text "Note"')
```

